WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.359 --> 00:00:03.060
When you're a cinephile, you get used to disappointment.

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:05.810
After all, Hollywood can't deliver the goods
all the time.

00:00:05.810 --> 00:00:09.910
Sure, Infinity War was pretty great, but we've
still got Justice League to deal with.

00:00:09.910 --> 00:00:13.820
Hunger Games might've torn it up at the box
office, but Divergent couldn't quite make

00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:14.940
it to the finish line.

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:17.780
Jurassic Park defined blockbuster cinema for
a generation.

00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:19.760
Its sequel, The Lost World: Jurassic Park?

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:20.920
Eh, not so much.

00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:25.890
That's how it's been forever, and yet it still
stings when a film fails to meet our expectations,

00:00:25.890 --> 00:00:30.010
especially when that means bungling a scene
that we've waited years, sometimes decades,

00:00:30.010 --> 00:00:31.560
to see unfold on the big screen.

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:36.310
Here's a not-so-fond look back at movie moments
that Hollywood failed to deliver.

00:00:36.310 --> 00:00:38.400
The Phoenix rises (and falls)

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:43.050
When Jean Grey gave her life at the end of
X2: X-Men United, sacrificing herself to save

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:46.820
her teammates, X-Men fans knew what was coming,
and they couldn't wait.

00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:51.290
After all, "The Dark Phoenix Saga" is considered
one of the best X-Men storylines of all time.

00:00:51.290 --> 00:00:53.340
Well, Fox and Brett Ratner didn't get the
memo.

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:57.230
In the comics, Jean Grey realizes that the
only way to keep the universe safe from the

00:00:57.230 --> 00:00:59.930
Dark Phoenix's power is by ending her own
life.

00:00:59.930 --> 00:01:02.560
And, yes, it's just as heartbreaking as it
sounds.

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:05.040
In X-Men: The Last Stand, that's not how things
go.

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:06.040
At all.

00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:10.430
Instead of using Cyclops as an emotional anchor,
Jean ends his life offscreen, early in the

00:01:10.430 --> 00:01:11.430
film.

00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:13.799
In the comics, the Dark Phoenix wreaks havoc
across space.

00:01:13.799 --> 00:01:17.680
In The Last Stand, she kinda just, well, stands
around looking moody.

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:21.729
When she kicks the bucket, it's Wolverine
who does the deed, not Jean herself, robbing

00:01:21.729 --> 00:01:25.510
the former Marvel Girl of both her agency
and her most heroic moment.

00:01:25.510 --> 00:01:27.340
It's not a tragically inevitable ending.

00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:30.700
It's a huge waste of audiences' time and money.

00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:32.130
A Stark reality

00:01:32.130 --> 00:01:33.270
Iron Man, A.K.A.

00:01:33.270 --> 00:01:37.900
Tony Stark, makes up for a lack of memorable
minor baddies by butting heads with one utterly

00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:39.299
evil arch-nemesis.

00:01:39.299 --> 00:01:41.640
His name is the Mandarin, and he's bad news.

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:45.940
With a bunch of magic rings that grant him
special abilities, he's a real thorn in Stark's

00:01:45.940 --> 00:01:47.560
side, in the comics, at least.

00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:51.670
While the Mandarin was originally slated to
appear in Iron Man numero uno, he was written

00:01:51.670 --> 00:01:55.369
out of the movie shortly before filming in
order to streamline the plot.

00:01:55.369 --> 00:01:59.360
That's why ol' Shellhead's biggest fans were
excited when Iron Man 3 rolled around and

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:03.549
it looked like Tony Stark's number one bad
guy was going to make his long-awaited debut.

00:02:03.549 --> 00:02:04.549
Surprise!

00:02:04.549 --> 00:02:05.950
That's not what happened at all.

00:02:05.950 --> 00:02:09.600
Instead, Stark learns that Ben Kingsley's
Mandarin is just an actor playing a part.

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:11.140
"Don't hurt the face!

00:02:11.140 --> 00:02:12.520
I'm an actor!"

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:13.520
Fans weren't pleased.

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:17.900
In fact, the backlash was so intense that
Marvel "apologized" by releasing a short film

00:02:17.900 --> 00:02:20.540
that reveals the "real" Mandarin is still
out there.

00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:21.540
That guy, though?

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:23.220
He still hasn't showed up on-screen.

00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:25.440
A Shire left unscoured

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:30.140
Here's a fun bit of literary trivia: The Lord
of the Rings saga doesn't end with Sauron's

00:02:30.140 --> 00:02:31.140
defeat.

00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:34.970
In The Return of the King's penultimate chapter,
"The Scouring of the Shire," Frodo, Sam, Merry,

00:02:34.970 --> 00:02:39.200
and Pippin return home to bands of ne'er-do-wells
roaming the streets and machines belching

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:41.340
black smoke into the sky.

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:45.380
After realizing this was the handiwork of
disgraced wizard Saruman, Frodo and his friends

00:02:45.380 --> 00:02:48.459
have no choice but to fight back, and it is
badass.

00:02:48.459 --> 00:02:53.050
Unfortunately, Peter Jackson's big-screen
adaptation ignores "The Scouring of the Shire's"

00:02:53.050 --> 00:02:54.050
action-packed ending.

00:02:54.050 --> 00:02:58.160
Instead, it relegates the whole event to a
brief vision that Frodo sees when he looks

00:02:58.160 --> 00:02:59.459
into the mirror of Galadriel.

00:02:59.459 --> 00:03:03.849
OK, let's be real: "The Scouring of the Shire"
isn't exactly a crowd-pleaser, but it's an

00:03:03.849 --> 00:03:05.430
important part of the story.

00:03:05.430 --> 00:03:08.780
Without it, The Return of the King ends on
a tiresome string of happy endings.

00:03:08.780 --> 00:03:09.780
Oh well.

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:12.360
At least Saruman got his just desserts in
the deleted scenes.

00:03:16.270 --> 00:03:17.500
Cursed cameos

00:03:17.519 --> 00:03:21.510
It's easy to forget these days, with the Pirates
of the Caribbean franchise focusing heavily

00:03:21.510 --> 00:03:26.150
on Johnny Depp's wacky Jack Sparrow, but Orlando
Bloom's Will Turner was once the lead character

00:03:26.150 --> 00:03:27.150
of the flicks.

00:03:27.150 --> 00:03:31.800
As time went on, however, his character became
less and less important to the Pirates franchise.

00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:36.180
In fact, he didn't appear in On Stranger Tides,
the fourth entry in the series, at all.

00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:40.720
So, as you can imagine, Pirates fans were
rightfully excited to see Bloom's big return

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:44.090
in the franchise's fifth installment, Dead
Men Tell No Tales.

00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:45.269
Then they saw the movie.

00:03:45.269 --> 00:03:48.629
As it turns out, Bloom's role is little more
than a glorified cameo.

00:03:48.629 --> 00:03:52.239
He appears at the beginning to introduce the
McGuffin, and then returns at the end for

00:03:52.239 --> 00:03:53.569
a brief conclusion.

00:03:53.569 --> 00:03:57.970
The real heavy lifting is done by Brenton
Thwaites, who plays Turner's son, and Depp,

00:03:57.970 --> 00:04:00.959
who'll keep making these things as long as
Disney keeps paying him.

00:04:00.959 --> 00:04:02.720
It was a major disappointment.

00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:06.940
Still, Bloom fared better than Knightley:
while she's got a cameo in the film as well,

00:04:06.940 --> 00:04:09.459
she doesn't get a single line of dialogue.

00:04:09.459 --> 00:04:11.060
Buzzkill

00:04:11.060 --> 00:04:14.290
Taken together, the two Kill Bill movies run
for 247 minutes.

00:04:14.290 --> 00:04:18.139
You know how long it takes the Bride to actually
end Bill's life?

00:04:18.139 --> 00:04:19.199
24 seconds.

00:04:19.199 --> 00:04:20.259
That's it.

00:04:20.259 --> 00:04:24.639
It takes more than 11 times that for the Bride
to whack Vernita Green, the first victim on

00:04:24.639 --> 00:04:25.759
her revenge tour.

00:04:25.759 --> 00:04:29.979
Daryl Hannah's Elle lasts for over five minutes
before the Bride plucks her other eye out.

00:04:29.979 --> 00:04:34.759
The fight against O-Ren Ishii's yakuza enforcers
at the House of Blue Leaves goes on for nearly

00:04:34.759 --> 00:04:39.159
seven minutes, and it takes just under five
to put O-Ren herself down for good.

00:04:39.159 --> 00:04:43.490
Basically, Kill Bill's entire hook is its
kinetic, hyper-stylized, and lengthy action

00:04:43.490 --> 00:04:47.309
scenes, and yet when it comes to the most
important one-on-one battle in the story,

00:04:47.309 --> 00:04:51.099
the one that both films are named after — it's
over in the blink of an eye.

00:04:51.099 --> 00:04:54.860
As far as the flick's characters and themes
go, it's a fine ending that demonstrates the

00:04:54.860 --> 00:04:56.970
Bride's conflicted relationship with Bill.

00:04:56.970 --> 00:05:00.559
Viewers who wanted the movie to end with a
fight worthy of the series, however, couldn't

00:05:00.559 --> 00:05:02.039
help but want a little more.

00:05:02.039 --> 00:05:04.119
Later, Vader

00:05:04.119 --> 00:05:08.199
Ask any Star Wars fanatic, and they'll set
the scene for you: Anakin, burned alive, lies

00:05:08.199 --> 00:05:09.509
on the operating table.

00:05:09.509 --> 00:05:13.710
He's betrayed the Jedi Order, and driven away
both his lover and his closest friend.

00:05:13.710 --> 00:05:15.039
He has nothing left.

00:05:15.039 --> 00:05:19.460
Slowly, the operation proceeds, and young
Skywalker is transformed into the devious

00:05:19.460 --> 00:05:20.460
Darth Vader.

00:05:20.460 --> 00:05:24.460
Broken, both physically and mentally, Vader
asks about Padmé's fate.

00:05:24.460 --> 00:05:26.689
The Emperor tells Vader that she has passed
on.

00:05:26.689 --> 00:05:29.089
And, worse, that Vader himself was responsible.

00:05:29.089 --> 00:05:33.919
Furious, the newly crowned Sith Lord breaks
free from his restraints, stumbles forward

00:05:33.919 --> 00:05:38.520
like a drunk bantha, raises his arms to the
sky, and screams, "Noooooo!"

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:41.379
And just like that, all the tension leaks
out.

00:05:41.379 --> 00:05:45.360
This is arguably the most important moment
in the prequel trilogy — if not the entire

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:49.789
franchise — and for some reason, George
Lucas decided to make his tragic villain react

00:05:49.789 --> 00:05:51.559
like a spoiled child.

00:05:51.559 --> 00:05:52.860
"NOT COOL.

00:05:52.860 --> 00:05:54.169
TOTALLY LAAAME."

00:05:54.169 --> 00:05:55.189
Thanks for watching!

00:05:55.189 --> 00:05:58.069
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:58.069 --> 00:06:00.279
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

